Cost does not equal time, or anything else, they are unit-less measures
 * Even in actual execution plans, costs are all an estimate
 * There are no "actual" equivalents for costs derived after queries execute

Costs are how we got to the plan we're looking at.

You tell SQL Server:
 * Which rows you want
 * Which columns you want
 * Which order you want

USE PassDataCommunitySummit2023;

SELECT TOP (100)
    a.company_name,
    a.contact_name,
    a.email_address,
    a.consulting_budget
FROM dbo.Attendees AS a
WHERE a.attendance_year = 2023
ORDER BY
    a.consulting_budget DESC;

Queries are just descriptions of what you *want* to see.
The optimizer figures out *how* best-enough to do it.

Sort of like how indexes contain data and stastistics describe data.
Databases contain data, and we describe what data we want to see.

Costs are based on a bunch of internal algorithms that SQL Server uses to
shape and choose execution plans for queries, in the hopes that
the costing mechanisms are correct-enough to get you a good-enough plan
to answer the question your query is asking quickly and efficiently.

Costing considers:
 * Rows (Cardinality Estimates from where/join, grouping, etc.)
 * I/O type (Random I/O like Key Lookups is costed higher than sequential I/O like scans)
 * CPU effort (Parallelism may reduce costs overall)
 * Memory requirements (Buffer Pool, etc.)

Cost is generalized
 * Based on one very old piece of hardware, not your specific hardware setup
 * https://erikdarling.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/10/2023-10-18_09-05-03.jpg
 * Meant to come up with good-enough plans on *any* set of hardware

All plan decisions are based on costs
 * Some costs are fixed per unit (CPU, I/O)

Other costs are based on statistics
 * Table size
 * Histograms
 * Uniqueness

Some things can really help the optimizer make better-enough plan choices:
 * Useful Indexes
 * SARGable Predicates
 * Unique Constraints
 * Value Constraints
 * Updated Statistics
 * Limiting query complexity
 * Avoiding things that don't have good costing support
   (XML, JSON, string splitting, user-defined functions, local variables, table variables)

*/

/*
I'm going to start this off with one of my favorite mis-costing demos

If I'm smart, I'll run this before getting started.

*/

DROP TABLE IF EXISTS
    #p1,
    #p2;

SELECT
    u.Id,
    u.DisplayName,
    p.*
INTO #p1
FROM dbo.Users AS u
OUTER APPLY
(
    SELECT
        Score = SUM(p.Score),
        AnswerCount = SUM(p.AnswerCount)
    FROM dbo.Posts AS p
    WHERE p.Id = u.Id
) AS p;

SELECT
    u.Id,
    u.DisplayName,
    p.*
INTO #p2
FROM dbo.Users AS u
OUTER APPLY
(
    SELECT
        Score = SUM(p.Score),
        AnswerCount = SUM(p.AnswerCount)
    FROM dbo.Posts AS p WITH (FORCESEEK) /*I am different*/
    WHERE p.Id = u.Id
) AS p;


/*
People always want to do ridiculous things, like
sort the plan cache or Query Store data by query cost to
find "expensive" queries to tune.

"Expensive" queries can be fast or slow.
"Inexpensive" queries can be fast or slow.

I'm not saying you can't make them faster, but... cost
is not the ultimate arbiter of query speed, or importance.

Okay Erik, so if costs are stupid, what should we look at in a query plan?

Look for queries with characteristics of poorly-performing computer-things:
 * High average/maximum CPU/duration
 * That run when users are most active
 * Usually run pretty often (this will vary)

Once you figure out what you *should* tune

(Not what you *want* to tune, which is usually different from what you *should* tune)

Get an *actual* execution plan, and look at
 * Operator times
 * Wait stats (or use sys.dm_exec_session_wait_stats, 2016+)
 * Estimates vs Actuals
 * Spills
 * Parallel thread distribution

No one will ever come to you and complain about logical reads their query is doing

This is a vanity metric.

*/





/*
Query optimization is driven by rules and heuristics

Early on, queries are mapped as tree structures

These structures are pattern matched to rules

Rules provide different logically equivalent plans
 * Placement of aggregates
 * Placement of predicates
 * Placement of joins

Look at all these glorious stallions.

*/

SELECT
    ts.name
FROM sys.dm_exec_query_transformation_stats AS ts
ORDER BY
    ts.name;


/*
When you get an actual execution plan, many operators
that required estimates for cost-based optimization
will have information added to help you figure out
how accurate those estimates were, and if any parts
of the chosen plan were chosen based on bad estimates

No actual costs, but there are:
 * Actual rows (read, all executions)
 * Actual batches (when Batch Mode is used)
 * Number of executions (pay attention to DOP)
 * Actual Rebinds/Rewinds (for Spools)

*/

SELECT
    c = COUNT_BIG(*)
FROM dbo.Votes AS v
WHERE v.BountyAmount = 501;


/*
Some estimates might look wrong, but are the
result of post-optimization rewrites to use
things like bitmaps, which can reduce rows
very early on when reading data

*/

WITH
    comment_count AS
(
    SELECT
        c.UserId,
        records = COUNT_BIG(*)
    FROM dbo.Comments AS c
    WHERE c.Score > 9
    GROUP BY
        c.UserId
)
SELECT TOP (100)
    u.DisplayName,
    cc.records
FROM dbo.Users AS u
JOIN comment_count AS cc
  ON cc.UserId = u.Id
ORDER BY
    u.Reputation DESC;


/*
The optimizer is biased against doing random I/O

Why? Because the reference hardware I showed you earlier
had crappy spinning disks in it, and when those things had
to do random I/O, it meant physical parts moving to locate data

Modern storage hardware like flash and SSDs doesn't have
those moving parts, but the costing algorithms haven't
really changed to understand that.

Probably because cloud storage is so god awful.

*/

EXEC dbo.DropIndexes;

